{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to her, etc.</b> This verse has drawn many interpretations, yet it still admits of further explanation and seasoning in a straightforward manner. For it comes to answer her concerning her worry that her pregnancy would not endure because of the great distress [of the children pressing each other], and He informed her of the cause of the distress — that they would press each other and struggle mightily one against the other — because this pregnancy differs from every pregnancy in the world. For every pregnancy of twins, the two are calm and their nature is the same, as it says (Song of Songs 7:4): ״תְּאוֹמֵי צְבִיָּה״, and they dwell together in the womb — but your pregnancy is not so. And the reason is <b>for two nations are in your belly</b>: each one of them has the stature of an entire nation, and furthermore they themselves are kings and their kingdom endures forever, and this is what it means by <b>and two peoples</b>. And none of this would cause struggling in the womb if they were of one temperament like the nature of twins; rather, they differ from the nature of twins, for from the very womb they are not at peace with one another, their temperament and nature being altered — and this is what it says: <b>from your womb they shall separate</b>. And it says <b>from your womb</b> (mi-me'ayikh) and not 'in your womb' (bi-me'ayikh), to say that they are always separated and the beginning of their separation is from your womb. And through the medium of this matter lies the wondrous change — not because her womb is unfit for a pregnancy of struggling children." "And His saying <b>and the one people shall be stronger than the other people</b>, etc. — meaning: there is yet another thing that will cause the distancing of any disposition toward harmony together. For each of the two kings — his strength and his might comes from the le'om that opposes him, after the manner of their statement, of blessed memory (Megillah 6a): 'Tyre was not filled except from the destruction of Jerusalem.' And since each of them desires his fellow's downfall in order to grow from it, this will push away the brotherhood and twinhood between them." "And His saying <b>and the elder</b>, etc. Interpretations of this have proliferated, yet none suffices for the truth of the verse. According to our approach it comes out correctly: the intention is to say that besides one growing great through his fellow's downfall, the fallen one must also enslave himself before the one who grows greater than him. And it is not enough for him that he falls and sees his good in his enemy's hand, but he must bend his head like a hook to serve him — and in this the estrangement of brotherhood and the intensity of hatred is pushed to the extreme. And concerning this it was said (Ketubot 72b): 'A man and a snake cannot dwell under one roof.' And this is the cause of the alteration of the pregnancy. And along these lines, His saying <b>and the elder shall serve the younger</b> — the intention in saying 'elder' and 'younger' is not specifically about Jacob or about Esav, but about whichever one is 'elder' in his time and whichever one is 'younger' in his time — like Kaftor and Vaferach."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:26.315367738Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}